---
import Layout from "../components/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Process — Jack Bush">
    <main>
        <section>
            <h1>How we engage</h1>
            <p>Most fractional relationships go wrong for one of two reasons: the scope is too vague, or the founder treats it like a contractor arrangement rather than a partnership.</p>
            <p>Here's how I work, so there are no surprises.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>What I'm here for</h2>
            <p>I'm not an extra pair of hands. I'm a thinking partner and decision-maker who can also get things done. The best engagements are ones where you treat me like a cofounder for the product — someone who's invested in getting it right, not just delivering to a brief.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Typical scope</h2>
            <ul>
                <li>1–2 days per week, minimum 3 months</li>
                <li>Mix of async (Slack, Notion, Figma review) and live time (weekly call, workshops when needed)</li>
                <li>I work across the full stack of early-stage product: research, strategy, design, and enough code to be dangerous</li>
                <li>Remote by default. I'm based in New Zealand and work with clients globally. I don't do regular nights for European clients.</li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>What works well</h2>
            <p>Founders who have a clear vision but need someone to pressure-test it, shape the roadmap, and make sure the team is building the right thing. Pre-product, early product, or post-first-product-and-lost-the-thread.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>What doesn't</h2>
            <p>Mature product orgs who need a process designer or someone to manage a big design team. That's not where I add the most value.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Week 1</h2>
            <p>I don't do discovery decks. Week 1 looks like this:</p>
            <dl>
                <dt>Day 1–2 — listen</dt>
                <dd>I read everything. Product docs, user research, old Slack threads if you'll share them. I talk to you and anyone else who's close to the product. I'm building a picture of where things are, not forming opinions yet.</dd>

                <dt>Day 3–4 — map</dt>
                <dd>I write down what I think the core problem is, what the product is currently solving, and where those two things don't match. I share it with you. This is usually where the most useful conversation happens.</dd>

                <dt>Day 5 — agree on what to do first</dt>
                <dd>We leave week 1 with a clear first priority: the thing that, if we get it right, moves everything else. Not a roadmap. One thing.</dd>
            </dl>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Rates</h2>
            <p>I work on a monthly retainer. Rates depend on scope and time commitment — typically [£X,000–£Y,000/month for 1–2 days/week].</p>
            <p>For pre-seed founders with real constraints, I'm open to conversations about deferred payment or equity involvement where it makes sense.</p>
            <p>If you're not sure what you need, a single paid half-day workshop is a good way to find out.</p>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
